        {
          "id": "bd299997-9630-3cdb-94ba-c6b84ff805f4",
          "content": {
            "id": "bd299997-9630-3cdb-94ba-c6b84ff805f4",
            "contentType": "STORY",
            "title": "Bank of America Shares Finally Recover From 2008 Financial Crisis",
            "description": "",
            "summary": "Bank of America  notched a symbolic win on Friday when its stock closed at $55.14, the first record-high price for the country\u2019s second-biggest bank since before the 2008 financial crisis.  Like other banks that were damaged during the crisis, Bank of America has struggled to get its stock price back to the highs seen when  George W. Bush  was still president.  With roots in North Carolina as what was once called NationsBank, Bank of America was a dealmaking juggernaut in the 1990s and 2000s, snapping up storied banks such as California\u2019s Bank of America in 1998 (whose name it then took) and Fleet Bank in 2003.",
